Evaluation of Speed of Sound and Specific Heat Capacities of Real Gases
Bahtiyar A. Mamedov∗
Gaziosmanpaşa University, Tokat, Turkey and Elif Somuncu† and Iskender M. Askerov‡ Giresun University, Giresun, Turkey
DOI: 10.2514/1.T5285
In this paper, by use of the second virial coefficient (12-6) in the Lennard–Jones potential, an analytical procedure to evaluate the specific heat capacity and speed of sound of gases is presented. The obtained formulas yield accurate and fast evaluation of both quantities. By using obtained analytical expressions, the heat capacity and speed of sound of the gases Ar, He, Xe, H2, O2, F2, Cl2, NF3, BCl3, Ga CH3 3, CO2, and air were calculated in the temperature range from 100 to 2000 K and over the pressure range from 0.1 atm to approximately 15 atm. The computational results are in good agreement with the data available in the literature.
I. Introduction
S
TUDYING the thermodynamic and transport properties of gases is important for many industrial applications [1,2]. In addition, accurate evaluations of the thermodynamic and transport properties of the fluid are necessary for many chemical processes, to optimize equipment, in design of engineering systems, and in the semiconductor processing industry [2–5]. Note that evaluation of thermodynamic and transport properties of gases, liquids, and solids is still one of the main problems in science. It can be seen from the literature that these properties are widely investigated using both experimental and theoretical methods [6–13]. The thermodynamic properties of gases, liquids, and solids, necessary in the design and phase behavior calculations, are generally determined through the equations of state [14–19]. The equations of state are applicable to all kinds of gases within certain pressure and temperature ranges. It is well known that the virial equation of state, an alternative to the van der Waals equation of state, can be derived from statistical mechanical theory [20–25]. Note that the virial equation of state is a fundamental equation describing thermodynamic properties of gases at low to moderate densities [26,27]. The heat capacity and speed of sound are of fundamental importance in the study of various properties of gases. They are used in the engineering design of chemical processes. Examples include the generalization of heat exchanger and energy balance design calculations or in gas-phase chemical reactions. These data are essential for determining the energy necessary to bring the chemical reactants up to reaction temperature. Because the virial equations of state define the various thermodynamic properties of real gases, it is necessary to derive the practical formulas from them regardless of whether they include differential and integral operations. In this study, we present a new method to evaluate the heat capacity at constant volume and pressure, and the speed of sound, of real gases. For this purpose, we have established analytical formulas for the first and second derivatives of the second virial coefficient. Finally, the established formulas are
used to calculate the heat capacity at constant volume and pressure as well as the speed of sound of the gases Ar, He, Xe, H2, O2, F2, Cl2, NF3, BCl3, Ga CH3 3, CO2, and air.
