III. Numerical Results and Discussion
In this paper, we propose an efficient approach to calculating the heat capacity at constant volume, heat capacity at constant pressure, and the speed of sound, virial coefficient with the Lennard–Jones (126) potential. The obtained formulas are completely general and free of any restrictions in terms of applications. To demonstrate the accuracy and efficiency of the analytical expressions, we present several calculations of properties for the gases Ar, He, Xe, H2, O2, F2, Cl2, NF3, BCl3, Ga CH3 3, CO2, and air. Based on Eqs. (12–14), we constructed programs for computation of the heat capacity at constant volume, heat capacity at constant pressure, and the speed of sound, using Pascal 7.0 international mathematical software. The heat capacities and speed of sound were determined in the temperature range from 100–2000 K and over the pressure range from 0.1 atm to approximately 15 atm. The calculated results were compared with experimental and theoretical data from the literature. The parameters σ, b0, ε∕kB, and M are used to describe
